Theorem 1: Suppose $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are real functions. Then:
(1) $\Delta C = 0$ (where $C$ is a constant);
(2) $\Delta(\lambda f(x) + \mu g(x)) = \lambda \Delta f(x) + \mu \Delta g(x)$, where $\lambda$ and $\mu$ are constants;
$$
\begin{array}{l}
\text{(3) } \Delta(f(x) g(x)) \\
= f(x) \Delta g(x) + g(x+1) \Delta f(x) \\
= f(x+1) \Delta g(x) + g(x) \Delta f(x).
\end{array}
$$

Definition 2: Suppose $f(x)$ is a real function, and $\sigma$ and $\tau$ are difference operators, shift operators, or identity operators. Define:

$$
\begin{array}{l}
(\sigma \pm \tau) f(x) = \sigma f(x) \pm \tau f(x), \\
\sigma \cdot \tau f(x) = \sigma(\tau f(x)).
\end{array}
$$

Then $\sigma + \tau$, $\sigma - \tau$, and $\sigma \cdot \tau$ are called the sum, difference, and product of $\sigma$ and $\tau$, respectively.

